-- title:
--   One rational place reads all admissible functions at H₁ level
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ denotes `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`.
--
--   **Global data.** A prime $q$, a natural number $M'$ (nonzero) with $q \nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$. Further, a valuation subring $A \subseteq \bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$. A finset $W$ of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` — the subfield of Laurent series over the residue field $\kappa(A)$ generated by `jqModC` and its level-$M'$ rescaling `jqNModC` — over $\kappa(A)$, with `hW` asserting that $W$ consists exactly of the members of `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, the supersingular places; and a chosen element $s \in W$. The hypothesis `hle` asserts the inclusion `modularFunctionFieldBar M' ≤ fieldBar q M'`, i.e. the $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfield of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the full level-$M'$ modular function field is contained in the corresponding base change `xHFunctionFieldBar (q ^ 2 * M') (levelH q M')`.
--
--   **The constant reduction.** A datum $R_0$ of type `ConstantReduction A ↥(modularFunctionFieldBar M') (modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M')`: a valuation subring `R₀.integers` of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, a surjective residue homomorphism `R₀.residue` onto `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` with kernel the maximal ideal of `R₀.integers`, a map `R₀.placeMap` on places, together with the compatibilities recorded in that structure (membership of constants in `R₀.integers` is equivalent to membership in $A$; the residue of a constant is its $A$-residue; every nonzero element becomes, after scaling by a constant, an element of `R₀.integers` with nonzero residue; `placeMap` preserves degrees; and `placeMap` pushes forward the divisor of an element with nonzero residue to the divisor of its residue). The hypothesis `hR₀` requires that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that element lies in `R₀.integers` and its $R_0$-residue, viewed as a Laurent series over $\kappa(A)$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   **Local data over a subfield.** An intermediate field $k_0$ of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, and an element $\pi_0 \in k_0$ whose image lies in $A$. Write $A_0 :=$ `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`, the valuation subring of $k_0$ induced by $A$. The hypotheses require: $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`); its maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$ (`hunif`); $A_0$ is a henselian local ring (`hhens`); the residue field of $A_0$ is algebraically closed (`hres`); and every element of $A$ is congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to an element of $k_0$ lying in $A$ (`hκ`).
--
--   **Roots of unity and the level group.** An element $\xi \in k_0$ which is a primitive $(q\ell_g)$-th root of unity, together with `hιξ`: there is a ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi) = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell_g))$. A subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ which, by `hH₁`, is the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22) — the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ — with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g)^\times$.
--
--   **The function field and its chart.** An intermediate field $K$ of $k_0 \subseteq k_0((q))$ which by `hK` equals `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`, the $k_0$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise images of the level-$(q^2M')$, $H_1$-invariant rational function field over $\mathbb{Q}$; an $A_0$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with the tower $A_0 \subseteq k_0 \subseteq K$; and an element $j \in K$ whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image `coeffEmb ↥k₀ jq` of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant, assumed nonzero. Write $C :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel.chartAlgFin ↥A₀ ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L142) for the $A_0$-subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A_0[j]$.
--
--   **Variable-change and transport hypotheses.** Three functoriality hypotheses, stated for every commutative $A_0$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and every variable change $C$ over $T$: `hℓ`, that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma1Point W ℓg D`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma1Pow.html#L10) (the four clauses: $(x_P,y_P)$ satisfies the affine equation, $(W.\mathrm{pre}\Psi\,\ell_g)(x_P) = 0$, $x_Q = x_P$, $y_Q = y_P$) implies `IsGamma1Point (C • W) ℓg (D.variableChange C)`; `hM`, that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt W p k h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) (which is `W.IsTwoKernel h` if $p^k = 2$ and `W.IsCyclicGenKernel p k h` otherwise) implies the same predicate for $C \bullet W$ and the transported polynomial `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h`; and `hL`, that $h \mid$ `inLineMulPoly W ℓg n x` implies `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h ∣ inLineMulPoly (C • W) ℓg n (u^{-2}(x - r))` for every $d$.
--
--   Further, group laws $\mathcal{G}$ over $A_0$ with `h𝒢` (chord–tangent: for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, projective Weierstrass curve $W/T$ with unit discriminant, the relative group law $\mathcal{G}$ admits a points evaluation) and `h𝒢O` (the identity is the origin section, with the two coordinate conditions `χ (xOverY W) = 0` and `χ (zOverY W) = 0`); a level transport $\mathcal{T}$ of type `LevelTransport ↥A₀ 𝒢 q` with `h𝒯` asserting `IsSectionTransport`, the two clauses comparing the sections of a transported raw Drinfeld pair under variable change and under base change. Finally, `hVC` and `hCO` assert the existence, for every projective Weierstrass curve over an $A_0$-algebra, of graded ring homomorphisms between the graded rings `projModelGradingCR` realising a given variable change (`IsVariableChangeHom`) respectively a given $A_0$-algebra map on coefficients (`IsCoefficientHom`), each subject to the condition that the irrelevant ideal of the target is contained in the image of the irrelevant ideal of the source.
--
--   **The moduli package and its classification.** Let $\mathcal{R} :=$ [`WeierstrassCurve.DrinfeldGlobal.rigidDataH1Pow ↥A₀ ℓg M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassH1Pow.html#L65) and let $D := \mathcal{R}$`.toLevelModuliDatum` be the associated level moduli datum. Then: $P_0$, a `LevelModuliPackageAbs ↥A₀ D`, i.e. an $A_0$-algebra $P_0.B_0$ with a universal point of $D$ over it such that every point of $D$ over any $A_0$-algebra is induced by a unique $A_0$-algebra map from $P_0.B_0$; a point $x_0$ of $D$ over $K$ whose $j$-invariant `D.jOf x₀`, read as a Laurent series over $k_0$, equals [`ModularCurve.jqNModC ↥k₀ q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) (hypothesis `hx₀`); an $A_0$-algebra map `clC` from $P_0.B_0$ to the chart algebra $C$ such that, after inclusion into $K$, it agrees with the classifying map `P₀.classify x₀` (hypothesis `hclC`). Finally a ring homomorphism $\theta : \kappa(A_0) \to \kappa(A)$ compatible with the residue maps of $A_0$ and $A$ along the inclusion $A_0 \hookrightarrow A$ (hypothesis `hθ`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every maximal ideal $y$ of the chart algebra $C$ and every $A_0$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : C \to \kappa(A_0)$ whose zero set is exactly $y$ (that is, $\psi(c) = 0$ if and only if $c \in y$), there exists a place $r$ of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` over $\kappa(A)$ such that:
--
--   1. $r$ is rational, i.e. the structure map $\kappa(A) \to r$`.ResidueField` is surjective;
--
--   2. the element `jGeomGen (ResidueField A) M'`, namely `jqModC` viewed inside `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'`, lies in the valuation subring of $r$;
--
--   3. for every Laurent series $g$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ belonging to `modularFunctionFieldFull M'`, such that the corresponding element of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` (the coefficientwise image of $g$ over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$) lies in `R₀.integers`, and such that for every place $P$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ the inequality $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}$ of the image of `jq` implies $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}$ of the image of $g$ (here `Place.ord f` is minus the logarithm of the adic valuation of $f$, so the condition says that $g$ is regular wherever $j$ is regular), and such that moreover `coeffEmb ↥k₀ g` lies in $K$ and the resulting element of $K$ lies in the chart algebra $C$: the residue `R₀.residue` of that element of `R₀.integers` lies in the valuation subring of $r$, and
--   $$\theta\bigl(\psi(g^{\sharp})\bigr) = r.\mathrm{evalAt}\bigl(R_0.\mathrm{residue}\,\bar g\bigr),$$
--   where $g^{\sharp} \in C$ is the element of the chart algebra determined by $g$ and $\bar g$ is its class in `R₀.integers`, and `Place.evalAt` is the $\kappa(A)$-valued evaluation at $r$ (the residue at $r$ pulled back along the inverse of the structure map, and $0$ outside the valuation subring).
--
--   Thus one and the same rational place $r$ computes, through $\theta \circ \psi$, the values at $y$ of all admissible modular functions of level $M'$ simultaneously.
--
--   This is the $H_1$-level instance of the dictionary between $\kappa$-points of the integral chart of the level-$(q^2M')$ modular curve cut out by $j$ and rational places of the geometric level-$M'$ modular function field in characteristic $q$: a point of the chart, presented as a maximal ideal together with the corresponding residue character $\psi$, is shown to be realised by a single place $r$ regular at the geometric $j$-invariant, through which every admissible function is read off. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.moduliPoint_mk_eq_of_forall_apply_eq_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataH1Pow_of_tatePoint_pinGamma1`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.moduliPoint_mk_eq_of_forall_apply_eq_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataH1Pow_of_tatePoint_pinGamma1), in the identification of points of the special fibre at $q$ with moduli of Weierstrass curves carrying the relevant level structure.
